86133989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133989 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133989 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Complements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133989 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133989 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133989 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133989 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cf31c13c-070a-552d-91a4-1a67e47f24d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134658 #edb/ref 1061 20219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd572f85-b48d-5bec-90bb-6618a7fc0fc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134659 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134659 #edb/ref 1061 20220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74cfe2c6-a806-59db-9604-de65c6177317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134660 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134660 #edb/ref 1061 20221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64ed6cee-8c85-5d4d-97ee-35ae15ad1e64" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134661 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134661 #edb/ref 1061 20222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "261dd9cf-9401-5924-9e13-578c45a64439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134662 #edb/ref 1061 20223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134662 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c64ae3ad-aece-5b13-99de-d1ff9cb5fbfd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134662 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134721 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134721 #edb/ref 1061 21872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134721 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cad6aad8-f1f1-51f6-82ae-549193d8d909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134721 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135043 #edb/ref 1064 "If a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to all the vectors in a set $W,$ we say that $\\mathbf{u}$ is **orthogonal to $W,$** and we write $\\mathbf{u} \\perp W.$\n\nFor example, consider the set of vectors ![](images/b9/b986146bf3d9edbf85c9047aa0e0acac54b9dd962c5c1f1b2d1612a36444337b.svg) Can we find a *non-zero* vector ![](images/2b/2be010cc1a39d762cb78272a1c6f8b10574823fa549ba2e54066f15c1d312ff9.svg) that is orthogonal to both vectors in this set?\n\nWe know that if such a vector $\\mathbf u$ does exist, then the dot product of $\\mathbf u$ with each vector in the set will be zero:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo let's calculate these dot products:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the components of $\\mathbf{u},$ we just need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the system's matrix is already in echelon form and has $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we have $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain $x_1=0.$ \n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we get the vector\n\n![](images/4a/4aee36f32445ae5fe5286d7a5aa7600f1f7fc8449fb981509cbd999822408c90.svg)\n\nSo, we have found a vector $\\mathbf{u}$ that is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}_1$ and $\\mathbf{w}_2.$\n\n![](images/a6/a64d75a0f21a4238c4b297f890d4a3202917d831c9d80427cffcc75dcdf343a0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186135043 #edb/ref 1065 3616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f7fff6c9-9086-5eef-b56a-a87ba95ad93b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135044 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's consider a *subspace* $H.$ The set containing all vectors $\\mathbf{v}$ that are orthogonal to $H$ is called the **orthogonal complement** of $H,$ and we denote it by $H^{\\perp}.$\n\nFor example, the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is the line that passes through the origin and is perpendicular to the plane.\n\n![](images/7e/7eb817f84f5a9f642b5721559f41c2c99a44f234cd22b8cf6e820b7f5d61e262.png)\n\n  \n\nHow can we check whether a vector $\\mathbf v$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}?$ Sometimes, $H$ can contain many vectors (often infinitely many), so it may not be possible to check that $\\mathbf v$ is orthogonal to every individual vector in $H.$\n\nTo check whether a vector belongs to $H^{\\perp},$ we use the following theorem:\n\n*A vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $H^{\\perp}$ if it is orthogonal to a set of vectors that spans $H$. *\n\nIn other words:\n\n*If $H=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\},$ then $\\mathbf{v} \\in H^{\\perp}$ if and only if $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\\{ \\mathbf{h}_1,\\mathbf{h}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{h}_n\\}.$*\n\nFor example, if the 2D-plane $H$ passing through the origin in the three-dimensional space $\\mathbb{R}^3$ (shown below) is spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2,$ then the orthogonal complement $H^\\perp$ of $H$ consists of all vectors that are perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{h}_1$ and $\\mathbf{h}_2.$\n\n![](images/f8/f8cf96c9871966fb0a7a921b70229e4a17bb9f6dad1756986f0500c70be6ab95.png)\n\n  \n\nSome other important things to notice about $H^{\\perp}$ are as follows:\n\n- $H^{\\perp}$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n\n- $(H^{\\perp})^{\\perp} = H$\n\n- $H \\cap H^{\\perp} = \\{\\mathbf 0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135044 #edb/ref 1065 3617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "565f1aa6-6d92-5a0d-8035-62690c9f8e0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135044 #edb/ref 1067 "Orthogonal Complements" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186159751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186159749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159746 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1af22238-7121-5859-8bd5-bb780f5bcb32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159747 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159747 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159747 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8e49ff5-5c55-5932-a2f1-7a848d8e5168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159748 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{8}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159748 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159748 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f50a19d2-7686-5036-9fda-2c7cfb65685d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159749 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186159749 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921